From: Christopher Stenger <chris.h.stenger@gmail.com>
Subject: [PATCH] media: cedrus: reset a stuck engine from the job context

*** OUT OF patches/kernel/series: HARDENING WITH NO DEMONSTRABLE EFFECT. ***
***
*** Written for a wedge that turned out not to exist on the shipping stack.
*** The engine absorbs repeated stalls and clients killed mid-decode without
*** trouble -- measured, see below -- so this fixes nothing that can be shown
*** to happen, and by this project's standard that is not a shipping change.
*** The code path it hardens is real; the failure is not reachable today.

WHAT THIS WAS WRITTEN FOR, and what measurement then said.

The premise was that a client stalling or dying mid-decode takes video away
from the whole system until reboot -- observed as a storm of "Failed to setup
decoding job: -22" affecting every client, GStreamer included.

IT DOES NOT REPRODUCE ON THE SHIPPING STACK. Measured on a clean boot:
12 watchdog timeouts from stalled decodes, five clients SIGKILLed mid-decode,
three SIGKILLed while a stall was pending, and two SIGKILLed out of three
concurrent clients -- the engine was healthy after every one, and dmesg showed
ZERO setup failures. Every observation behind the original premise came from
runs with the work-in-progress resolution-change driver installed, which
re-sets the coded format mid-life; that driver, not the client crashes, is
what poisoned the engine.

THE MECHANISM IT HARDENS, which is real by inspection. cedrus_h265_setup() reads the engine's own bit reader while
building a job:

    cedrus_h265_skip_bits(dev, (slice_params->data_byte_offset - 1) * 8);
    padding = cedrus_h265_show_bits(dev, 8);
    if (padding == 0)
            return -EINVAL;

A VLD left stuck by a stalled decode reads back zero forever, so EVERY later
job fails there -- for every client, GStreamer included:

    cedrus 1c0e000.video-codec: Failed to setup decoding job: -22

and nothing puts the engine right. The watchdog resets only the "ve" line on
its own timeout path, and a stalled VLD has been observed to survive that;
stop_streaming stopped resetting anything when the halt-before-free patch was
dropped for deadlocking concurrent clients.

THE FIX IS WHERE, NOT WHAT. Reset the engine at the start of the next job,
gated on a flag set wherever the engine becomes suspect: a decode timeout, a
failed job setup, or a context tearing down mid-stream.

device_run() is the only safe place. A reset is device-wide, so doing it from
stop_streaming() breaks a job belonging to another context -- exactly how the
previous attempt deadlocked three concurrent decoders inside
v4l2_m2m_cancel_job(), a failure that needed a reboot to clear and whose own
patch comment had predicted it. In device_run() the m2m core has already given
this context the device and no other job can be in flight, so safety comes
from construction rather than from timing. That is the alternative the earlier
patch named and did not take.

Stateless decoding keeps no engine state between jobs -- every parameter set,
buffer address and control is programmed per job -- so a reset here costs
microseconds and loses nothing.

Behind sunxi_cedrus.job_reset, default on and writable at runtime, so both
behaviours can be compared inside one boot.

Signed-off-by: Christopher Stenger <chris.h.stenger@gmail.com>
---
--- a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus.h
+++ b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus.h
@@ -202,6 +202,14 @@
 	unsigned int		capabilities;
 
 	struct delayed_work	watchdog_work;
+
+	/*
+	 * Set when the engine may have been left in an indeterminate state --
+	 * a decode timeout, a failed job setup, or a context tearing down
+	 * mid-stream. Acted on at the start of the next job, which is the only
+	 * point where the device is known to be idle. See cedrus_device_run().
+	 */
+	bool			needs_reset;
 };
 
 extern struct cedrus_dec_ops cedrus_dec_ops_mpeg2;
--- a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_dec.c
+++ b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_dec.c
@@ -19,9 +19,21 @@
 #include <media/v4l2-mem2mem.h>
 
 #include "cedrus.h"
+#include <linux/moduleparam.h>
+#include <linux/reset.h>
+
 #include "cedrus_dec.h"
 #include "cedrus_hw.h"
 
+/*
+ * Runtime switch so both behaviours can be compared inside one boot, the way
+ * every other engine-state change in this driver has been.
+ */
+static bool job_reset = true;
+module_param(job_reset, bool, 0644);
+MODULE_PARM_DESC(job_reset,
+		 "reset the VE before a job when it may have been left stuck");
+
 void cedrus_device_run(void *priv)
 {
 	struct cedrus_ctx *ctx = priv;
@@ -92,12 +104,45 @@
 
 	v4l2_m2m_buf_copy_metadata(run.src, run.dst, true);
 
+	/*
+	 * Put the engine back to a known state before programming it, if
+	 * anything since the last job suggested it might not be in one.
+	 *
+	 * WHY HERE AND NOWHERE ELSE. A reset is device-wide, so doing it from
+	 * stop_streaming() breaks a job belonging to another context -- that
+	 * is exactly how the previous attempt (dropped) deadlocked three
+	 * concurrent clients inside v4l2_m2m_cancel_job(). device_run() is the
+	 * one place where the m2m core has already given this context the
+	 * device and no other job can be in flight, so the reset is safe by
+	 * construction rather than by timing.
+	 *
+	 * WHY IT IS NEEDED. cedrus_h265_setup() reads the engine's bit reader
+	 * (skip_bits/show_bits) while building a job and returns -EINVAL if it
+	 * reads back zero. A VLD left stuck by a stalled decode therefore
+	 * fails EVERY later job, for EVERY client -- GStreamer included --
+	 * until the module is reloaded or the board rebooted. One client
+	 * stalling or dying took video away from the whole system.
+	 *
+	 * Stateless decoding carries no engine state between jobs: every
+	 * parameter set, buffer address and control is programmed per job, so
+	 * a reset here costs a few microseconds and loses nothing.
+	 */
+	if (job_reset && dev->needs_reset) {
+		if (dev->rstc_ve3)
+			reset_control_reset(dev->rstc_ve3);
+		reset_control_reset(dev->rstc);
+		dev->needs_reset = false;
+	}
+
 	cedrus_dst_format_set(dev, &ctx->dst_fmt);
 
 	error = ctx->current_codec->setup(ctx, &run);
-	if (error)
+	if (error) {
 		v4l2_err(&ctx->dev->v4l2_dev,
 			 "Failed to setup decoding job: %d\n", error);
+		/* The engine is a candidate for having caused this. */
+		dev->needs_reset = true;
+	}
 
 	/* Complete request(s) controls if needed. */
 
--- a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_hw.c
+++ b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_hw.c
@@ -205,6 +205,14 @@
 		return;
 
 	v4l2_err(&dev->v4l2_dev, "frame processing timed out!\n");
+
+	/*
+	 * The reset below covers the "ve" line only, and a stalled VLD has
+	 * been observed to survive it -- every later job then fails in
+	 * cedrus_h265_setup() with -EINVAL. Mark the engine so the next job
+	 * resets it fully, from the one context where that is safe.
+	 */
+	dev->needs_reset = true;
 	reset_control_reset(dev->rstc);
 	v4l2_m2m_buf_done_and_job_finish(ctx->dev->m2m_dev, ctx->fh.m2m_ctx,
 					 VB2_BUF_STATE_ERROR);
--- a/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_video.c
+++ b/drivers/staging/media/sunxi/cedrus/cedrus_video.c
@@ -654,6 +654,15 @@
 	struct cedrus_dev *dev = ctx->dev;
 
 	if (V4L2_TYPE_IS_OUTPUT(vq->type)) {
+		/*
+		 * A context is leaving. It may have been mid-decode -- a
+		 * player killed, a client that crashed -- so the engine is
+		 * suspect. Flag it rather than resetting here: this runs on
+		 * the departing context's thread while another context may
+		 * have a job in flight.
+		 */
+		dev->needs_reset = true;
+
 		if (ctx->current_codec->stop)
 			ctx->current_codec->stop(ctx);
 
